<TopicPage slug="subjects/sports-science/expert/long-term-athlete-development">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    LTAD is the ladder\'s synthesis at the career scale: <strong>development as a decade-long
    programme</strong> — sampling before specialising, load matched to growth, and skill windows
    caught rather than missed. It is sports science\'s answer to "how to build an athlete
    without breaking the child."
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="windows, specialisation costs, growth-spurt care, the pathway" widget="equationStep" config={ltadCfg} />

  <h2>The expert\'s honest ledger</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Youth strength training is safe when coached:</strong> the old "stunts growth" myth is dead — supervised resistance training reduces injury rates in adolescent athletes (position statements agree); technique quality, not age, is the gate.</li>
    <li><strong>Relative age effect is measurable injustice:</strong> kids born just after the selection cutoff dominate youth squads (8–12 months older than peers) — the effect leaks into education and life outcomes; federations now rotate cut-offs and use bio-banding (grouping by maturation, not age) as correctives.</li>
    <li><strong>Mental skills belong in the pathway:</strong> psychological readiness (resilience, identity beyond sport) predicts both performance and healthy exit — the psychology lesson\'s development and motivation findings belong in the coach\'s plan.</li>
    <li><strong>Retirement is part of development:</strong> every elite career ends; identity foreclosure (athlete-as-whole-self) makes transitions brutal — the best programmes prepare the exit from the day of entry. The ladder ends where the person continues.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Balyi, Way & Higgs, Long-Term Athlete Development (2013); Côté\'s developmental model; relative-age-effect literature (Musch & Grondin).</p>
</TopicPage>
